st it but will he now given  that he's in favor of mass testing  introduce regular weekly testing of all  frontline nhs workers but to move to  mass testing  means evol evolving our testing regime  from one  which provides antibody tests and the  diagnostic pcr tests  to affect effectively a system of mass  screening  using more rapid on-the-spot antigen  tests  he referred to rapid tests in his  statement can he tell us  when rapid on-the-spot antigen tests  will be rolled out  across society and which sectors of the  workforce  will be first in the queue to access  those rapid on-the-spot  antigen tests well they now also look at  introducing saliva testing it's being  used  in hong kong for example it will ensure  the quick turnaround of tests  and i wonder if he's seen the street  street from yale that  suggests saliva testing could be as  sensitive  as the nose and throat swabs what is his  attitude towards  pool testing because pool testing would  surely increase the capacity  in areas of low prevalence does he have  a plan to introduce  pooled testing he's right that we are  investing in public health teams and so  we should  and as we discussed in questions it's  important to have the combination  of the the national system and  the local it's also very important that  we communicate  to people that it's important to engage  in testing  and the contact tracing for those who  are who test positive in their contacts  and so being able to communicate to  people so that they get those messages  is very important  and we'll do that in whatever way is  effective to get those messages  across um he asks about the importance  of mass testing  um i have been i bow to no one  in my enthusiasm for mass testing i'm  very glad that he  supports my drive for mass testing uh  he's  um he might remember the exchanges that  we had some time ago when  i rather stuck my neck out in pushing  for mass testing  when we needed to get to hundreds of  thousands of tests well we need to  increase the number of tests again um he  mentioned both saliva tests and pool  tests  uh we're trialing both of those uh but  as with vaccines which i'll come on to  briefly mr speaker we will only use  testing  that is validated and that is where the  results are  safe so it's important that we use  the the world-class facilities that we  have at port and down  to make sure that tests are validated  before we use them in public  but saliva testing and pool testing are  both both  options that we are that we are working  on  when it comes to local lockdowns mr  speaker local lockdowns are  working this local action again jointly  between national and local government  is having an effect and he knows this  well from leicester where the  case rate is right down we absolutely do  publish the data on which these  decisions are made  and in fact from last thursday now  published data  at lower super output area which is the  lowest level in terms of how local the  test results can be  reasonably published and we have  extensive data provided to directors of  public health
 

